<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grace Contract</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grace Contract was an agreement signed in 1888 and ratified in 1889 between the Peruvian government and its British bondholders, negotiated by Michael Grace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s foreign debt was cancelled in exchange for control of the state railways for 66 years, guano shipments, annual payments and land concessions. The bondholders formed the Peruvian Corporation to run the railways, and the deal restored Peru's credit after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> had run up heavy debts since independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, largely through loans raised in 1865, 1866, 1869, 1870 and 1872 with the help of the Dreyfus firm and the Guano Syndicate. The loan of 1872, of £36,800,000, was meant to retire earlier loans, but the government had reportedly authorised President Balta to raise only £9.3 million, and the London Stock Exchange Committee restricted it to that amount. Relations with the Syndicate soured under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-pardo">Manuel Pardo</a>, and on 18 December 1875 Peru defaulted on its bonds; normal payments were never resumed.</p><p>Defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> made matters worse. The war cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> territories rich in guano and nitrates, exports that had brought in about 70 per cent of its customs revenue, and left the government little leverage with its creditors. Michael Grace, who with his brother William Russell Grace had founded Grace Brothers and Co. in 1865, had a large guano trade and a shipping line, and together with Lord Donoughmore he secured the ratification of the contract that bears his name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The settlement involved the Peruvian government, the Chilean government and the British bondholders, whose Peruvian Corporation took on the obligations meant to recoup their losses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s debt was cancelled in return for annual payments of £80,000, mining rights and ownership of the Peruvian railways for 66 years, and the corporation undertook to build 160 kilometres of new line. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> conceded to the bondholders the guano deposits of Huanillos, Punta de Lobos, Pabellón de Pica and Lobos de Afuera, gave up its claim to 80 per cent of the guano money held in English banks, about £1,000,000, and halted its own guano sales for four years: concessions worth £2,250,000 in all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The contract confirmed British financial dominance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and brought a new flow of investment that quickened the country's recovery, above all through the rehabilitation of the railways and their extension to mining centres such as La Oroya. It did not settle the debts of the Guano Syndicate, whose leader, J. T. North, died in 1896 without seeing them repaid. Those claims went to an international tribunal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> in 1901, and a further payment followed a decision at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> in 1921.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grace Contract” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
